{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-337053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/2cccb2a1edfeed0a562303053395c6599e14cbf08f9dc5e7f07a4d657755b1ae.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $250\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.004\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=250\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=250I=250\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15=250\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+q=\\frac{3}{50}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-337053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q=\\frac{3}{50}"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1}{250}q=30"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{3}{50}q=1"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+q=30"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+q=\\frac{3}{50}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dq}{dt}+q=\\frac{3}{50}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $50\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.002\\,\\text{F}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-337062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+50q=\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $80\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.00125\\,\\text{F}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=80\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=80I=80\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.00125}=800q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=80\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+800q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-337063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+800q=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+800q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+80q=\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.01\\,\\text{F}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{10}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-337039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q=9"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q=\\frac{3}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=\\frac{10}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{10}{3}q=9"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{10}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{10}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/6f9417fdec3a123dbb28ae87a31c6dc49e38327178e1335030b51505f5ca2cbd.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $60\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.003\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=60\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=60I=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.003}=\\frac{1000}{3}q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-337104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q=18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{9}{50}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =\\frac{2}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{2}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{15}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{2}{15}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{2}{15}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{2}{15}$ coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337111" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-337111" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337111" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{15}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $18\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.006\\,\\text{F}.$ Find, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.05$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{90}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=18I=18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.006}=\\frac{500}{3}q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9=18\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{500}{3}q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{250}{27}q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{250}{27}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-250t/27},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{250}{27}\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{27}{500}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{27}{500}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-250t/27}+\\frac{27}{500}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{90}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{90},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{90} \\\\ A{e}^{-250(0)/27}+\\frac{27}{500} & =\\frac{1}{90} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{193}{4500}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{27}{500}-\\frac{193}{4500}{e}^{-250t/27}\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.05$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.05)=\\frac{27}{500}-\\frac{193}{4500}{e}^{-250(0.05)/27}≈0.027,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337124" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-337124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.011"] ["math" "0.020"] ["math" "0.027"] ["math" "0.038"] ["math" "0.054"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337124" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.027"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/b91e3582e017882aae67e8b6378074cf941be0c090d9f3f978076583b36c8837.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{25}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{25}{4}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{25}{4}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/4},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{25}{4}\\alpha =\\frac{3}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{6}{125}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{125}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/4}+\\frac{6}{125}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{25}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{25},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)/4}+\\frac{6}{125} & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{125}{e}^{-25t/4}+\\frac{6}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{125}(6-{e}^{-25t/4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{1}{125}{e}^{-25t/4}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{6}{125}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-337114" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337114" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.048-0.008e^{-6.25t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-336594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+6q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-336594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+6{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{1}{12}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{1}{12}$ coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-336594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-336594" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-336594" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{12}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-336590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\nFind, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.05$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{80}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-336590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}q=\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{25}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{5}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{40}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{40}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3}+\\frac{3}{40}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{80}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{80},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{80} \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)/3}+\\frac{3}{40} & =\\frac{1}{80} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{3}{40}-\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-25t/3}\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.05$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.05)=\\frac{3}{40}-\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-25(0.05)/3}≈0.034,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-336590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-336590" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-336590" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.009"] ["math" "0.028"] ["math" "0.034"] ["math" "0.045"] ["math" "0.071"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-336590" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.034"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/0bf264310dba0dc66332b3c8e0f2e1c780ca199e7d5ea3c1f21f5132a02e06dd.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{200})}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n75\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+8{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +8\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{9}{50}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{6}{25})}^{2}+{(\\frac{9}{50})}^{2}}=0.3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-337349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.2"] ["math" "0.25"] ["math" "0.3"] ["math" "0.4"] ["math" "0.45"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/b1933db5df10c1a3faf07f1d459a7736be682a3d01ab85e48214da21e7a9d1d5.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{100})}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(5\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +5\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{16}{41},\\beta =\\frac{20}{41}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{16}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{20}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{16}{41})}^{2}+{(\\frac{20}{41})}^{2}}≈0.62,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-337352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.55"] ["math" "0.58"] ["math" "0.62"] ["math" "0.66"] ["math" "0.72"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337352" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.62"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/f1568a632a02f644f7ef8c39417bb06973f0862494aa0c3c624880a87c5620ee.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $25\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=25\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=25I=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{100})}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=25\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $5t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $5t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+(4\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +4\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{10}{41},\\beta =\\frac{8}{41}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{41}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{8}{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{10}{41})}^{2}+{(\\frac{8}{41})}^{2}}≈0.31,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-337348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.26"] ["math" "0.30"] ["math" "0.31"] ["math" "0.36"] ["math" "0.41"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.31"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/0221452e6c995bd4d4e027452c9949d57afc181e99d549434fc48ce8e29ced4b.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{P}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{Q}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.004\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{29},\\beta =\\frac{2}{145}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=1,Q=2,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=1+2=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e178470d5a12462d83952bf316a2120900ae53a121896e56363f035cea9ab43d"} {"question" "q-337138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/state.json" "file_sha256" "58e31512918eb01ee0b37cca6ea8355c0d8d36b6556d2a6392a16f89b46c90f5"} {"question" "q-337350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/a